          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Righteousness broadly means rightness according to God, encompassing moral conformity and just conduct as well as, especially in salvation contexts, right standing before God granted through Christ. Traditions dispute whether that standing is imputed, infused, covenantal, or transformative.\",\"book_summary\":\"Righteousness is right-living: God's own ways are inherently right, and human righteousness under his authority consists in living out his commandments. The book contrasts this with the common prioritization of imputed right standing.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Rightness or conformity relative to God, including both right standing and right conduct.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Conduct that enacts God's commandments—right-living under God's authority.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book retains the consensus category of conduct conforming to God's will but makes enacted obedience definitional. It excludes or reclassifies cases in which a person is called righteous solely by credited, forensic, or covenantal standing apart from right-living; the common view treats such standing as righteousness itself and right-living as its fruit.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"At the disputed boundary, the consensus permits imputed right standing itself to constitute righteousness, whereas the book identifies righteousness with lived obedience. This is a substantive disagreement, but not a wholesale replacement of the broader category of rightness according to God.\",\"rationale\":\"The book's identification is recognizable within the consensus's ethical and transformative dimensions, so it is neither novel nor wholly divergent. However, it materially narrows the term from a category covering both status and conduct to conduct—specifically commandments lived out. The central shared referent is alignment with God's standard, while forensic status without corresponding right-living is reclassified or excluded.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Romans 4:4–8 and Philippians 3:9, printed with the entry, explicitly speak of righteousness counted or supplied by God apart from one's works or law-derived righteousness. Although Deuteronomy 6:25, Psalm 119:172, and 1 John 3:7 strongly support an obedience-based use, the citation set does not by itself establish that right-living exhausts the meaning of righteousness.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
